statement:
You get two arrays that are indexed from 1: `nums` of length `n` and `changeIndices` of length `m`. Every index of `nums` starts unmarked, and the goal is to mark all of them.

Time runs in seconds `s = 1, 2, ..., m`. During second `s` you may do exactly one of these:

- pick any index `i` (1 to `n`) and lower `nums[i]` by 1;
- overwrite `nums[changeIndices[s]]` with any non-negative integer you like;
- pick an index `i` whose `nums[i]` is currently 0 and mark it;
- do nothing.

Acting as well as possible, return the smallest second `s` (between 1 and `m`) by whose end every index can be marked, or `-1` if that never happens within the `m` seconds.

validator:
function validate([nums, changeIndices]) {
  const n = nums.length;
  if (n < 1 || n > 5000) return 'nums needs 1 to 5000 values.';
  if (changeIndices.length < 1 || changeIndices.length > 5000) return 'changeIndices needs 1 to 5000 values.';
  if (nums.some(v => !Number.isInteger(v) || v < 0 || v > 1e9)) return 'Each nums value is an integer from 0 to 10^9.';
  if (changeIndices.some(v => !Number.isInteger(v) || v < 1 || v > n)) return 'Each changeIndices value is an index from 1 to nums.length.';
  return null;
}

